#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Judge the repository that is published, not one this Mac stands up.

`tools/test-apt-repository` builds a repository, serves it from this Mac and
asks apt what it makes of it. Everything it proves is true of the builder. What
it cannot reach is the half that only exists in production: R2 holding the
bytes, `workers/release-registry` handing them out, and the archive key that
signed them rather than a key generated for the run.

So this points one disposable arm64 Debian guest at the published repository
and runs the line a person is given, which fetches `install.sh` from the web
site as well. Nothing here substitutes an address.

    tools/test-published-apt-repository --suite trixie-testing
    tools/test-published-apt-repository --suite trixie-testing --disturb-published-objects
    tools/test-published-apt-repository --suite trixie-testing --prove-upgrade <newer.deb>

Two of the three refusals need the repository to be wrong, and the only place
it can be wrong is the bucket. `--disturb-published-objects` is what writes:
the suite's signatures are taken away and put back, and the .deb is replaced by
a same-length copy carrying one flipped byte and then put back. Without the
flag those two observations are not made, and the run says so rather than
passing.

`--prove-upgrade` is the other thing that writes, and what it writes is a
release. The guest installs what the suite carries, is given a company so the
services are running and can be asked what they are, and only then is the newer
package published beside the one already there. Then `apt-get upgrade` runs and
the same services are asked again. dpkg reporting the new version is not the
claim: a unit that is not restarted serves the old binary while dpkg, the
file's mtime and apt all say otherwise, so what decides it is the revision the
running process reports at `admind`'s health endpoint.

The older package is downloaded from the suite and republished beside the newer
one, so a machine pinned to the version it has does not stop being able to
fetch it.
"""

